WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
You type a prompt on your laptop and hit send.

2
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:09.250
We skip the network trip to the server. Every number from here on is measured on the GPU server.

3
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:15.180
On the server's CPU, a tokenizer splits the prompt into 512 tokens.

4
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:21.320
Each token ID picks a row of 5,120 numbers from the embedding table.

5
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:26.890
Stacked together, that's a 512 × 5,120 matrix.

6
00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:31.360
In the prompt phase, the whole matrix enters layer 1 at once.

7
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:39.890
Layer 1 is a chain of small GPU programs called kernels. The CPU sends them, and the GPU runs them in order.

8
00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:47.680
The Q, K, V matrix multiply saves the keys and values of all 512 tokens in the KV cache.

9
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:53.870
Matrix multiplies (GEMMs) take 92.6% of the GPU's time.

10
00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:59.530
Each layer's output becomes the next layer's input, through all 64 layers.

11
00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:06.210
Then the output head picks the first token. The whole prompt phase took 54.3 ms.

12
00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:14.740
Now decode begins. The KV cache already holds those 512 tokens, so only the new token goes back in.

13
00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:25.240
That's 902 kernels and 20.55 ms per token, about 48.7 tokens a second, for every token that follows.
